# Agent boundaries (migrations, git, deployment)

## Migrations

- **Never** apply or roll back migrations: no `make migrate`, `make rollback`, or anything that changes database state.
- **Creating a revision**: may run `make create-migration name='<revision message>'` **only after the user explicitly approves** in chat. Then implement `upgrade()` / `downgrade()` in the generated file under `alembic/versions/`. See **migrations.mdc** for revision content rules.
- Do not run `make create-migration` without that approval.

## Git

- **Never**: `git add`, `git commit`, `git push`, `git checkout`, or any command that stages, commits, or changes version-control state.
- **Allowed (read-only, for context)**: `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git show`, and similar commands that only read history or working tree.

## ADRs (Architecture Decision Records)

- **Only the user** creates or edits ADRs under `docs/adr/`. Agents must **not** run `make create-adr`, write new ADR files, or edit existing ADRs.
- Agents **may read** ADRs and must follow them when implementing or proposing changes.
- The **explain agent** may **recommend** that the user create an ADR (with a suggested title and what to capture)—see **explain-agent.md**.

## Dependencies (Poetry)

- **Never**: `poetry add`, `poetry remove`, `poetry update`, `poetry install`, `poetry lock`, or editing `pyproject.toml` / `poetry.lock` to add or bump packages.
- **User runs** Poetry when a new package is needed; the agent suggests the exact command (`poetry add …` or `poetry add --group dev …`) and reminds the user to run `make build` (Docker Compose) or `make build-container` (K8s local) after dependency changes.
- Full workflow: **dependencies.mdc**.

## Deployment / environment

- **Never**: `make start`, `make stop`, `make build`, `make build-container`, or anything that alters the environment, deploys, or persists data—unless the user explicitly asks.
- **Exception (quality checks)**: agents **must** run **`make agent-checks`** after producing or changing code. That target uses **`agent-ensure-env`**, which may run `docker compose up -d api database` when those services are stopped. Do **not** call `make start` or `make build` yourself for checks—only `make agent-checks` (optionally `make agent-lint` or `make agent-test` with `t=` if you need a single step).
- **Allowed (read-only)**: `make list-containers`, `docker compose ps`, `make logs` — to verify Compose state. See **docker-compose.mdc**.
